{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nBefore any human go/no-go on a production swap, the reclaim has to be shown end to end on a throwaway copy of the store. The epic predicts that a 30-day horizon cuts the blobless `tickets` pack to about 365–380 MiB. The CI guard currently measures 648,416 KiB against a temporary 563,200 KiB budget (epic comment of 2026-09-18). This story builds the dry-run harness and produces the evidence packet that goes to the human.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a new module, `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_dry_run.py`, runnable as `python -m rebar._commands.reclaim_dry_run --source <repo-or-url> --workdir <empty dir> [--horizon-days N] [--sweep-retired]`. It chains the merged pieces and never writes to `--source`:\n\n1. **Copies.** `git clone --no-local --branch tickets <source> <workdir>/t0`, a read-only fresh clone. Its tip is T0. Then `git clone --bare` of that copy gives `<workdir>/fake-remote.git`, the throwaway stand-in for the published remote. The shadow is a clone of the fake remote with its `origin` removed, the `.rebar/reclaim-shadow-clone` marker written, and `refs/remotes/origin/tickets` set to T0. That gives S1's horizon check its anchor (`_require_remote_horizon_ref`, `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_collapse.py:238`).\n2. **Boundary.** The newest first-parent commit whose committer date is at or before `now − horizon_days`, from `git rev-list -1 --first-parent --before=<cutoff> T0`.\n3. **Before.** The reduced state comes from S1's `reduce_shadow_tracker`. The pack size is the `size-pack` that `git count-objects -v` reports for a `git clone --bare --filter=blob:none` of the fake remote. That is the same blobless shape the CI guard measures.\n4. **Rewrite and publish.** `collapse_shadow_tracker(..., apply=True)` runs, with `sweep_retired` passed through, followed by `stamp_superseding_epoch` and `publish_git_ref` to `file://<fake-remote>` under a T0 lease (`src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_publish.py`).\n5. **After.** Three checks run on the result: the reduced state must equal the before state; `python -m rebar verify-identity --all` runs with `REBAR_TRACKER_DIR=<shadow>` and `REBAR_IDENTITY_ENFORCE_SINCE=<result.enforce_since>`, as S1's test does; and the blobless pack of the fake remote is measured again.\n6. **Packet.** `<workdir>/evidence.json` records the source, T0, horizon, boundary, new tip, commit counts, `pack_kib_before`/`pack_kib_after`/`delta_kib`, the predicted 365–380 MiB band, `reducer_equal`, the `verify_identity` exit code and output tail, `source_tip_unchanged`, and the time each step took. The command exits non-zero if any check fails.\n\nThe S3 bundle adapter is covered by S2's fake-S3 tests and is not repeated here. It works the same way across backends.\n\nThis story depends on `silk-unpleasant-jackal`. Without that change, S1 replays the 13,119 commits of the last 30 days at about 221k subprocess calls each, and a real-size run never finishes.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_dry_run.py` (new): the harness and evidence packet.\n- `tests/unit/test_reclaim_dry_run.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\n\nTDD, red first, on a synthetic store under `tmp_path` (CREATE/COMMENT events, a SNAPSHOT with `.retired` sources, and commits on both sides of a horizon).\n\n- Happy path: the packet records `reducer_equal: true`, `verify_identity` exit 0, `pack_kib_after <= pack_kib_before`, and `source_tip_unchanged: true`. The fake remote's `tickets` equals the new tip.\n- Failure path: a source with no commit older than the horizon makes the harness exit non-zero and write a packet whose `error` names \"no eligible boundary\". The source stays untouched.\n- Safety: the harness refuses a `--workdir` that is not empty.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The harness runs S1 and S2 against throwaway clones and a fake remote, with no write to the source. `tests/unit/test_reclaim_dry_run.py` asserts `source_tip_unchanged` and the fake remote's new tip.\n- [ ] It asserts that `verify-identity --all` is clean and that reducer state is equal after the rewrite, as shown by the happy-path test.\n- [ ] It writes a machine-readable `evidence.json` with the before and after blobless pack sizes and the delta against the epic's prediction, as shown by the happy-path test.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The harness is run once against a read-only clone of the real `tickets` branch, and its `evidence.json` summary is posted on this ticket and escalated to a human for the pre-swap go/no-go. No production swap is executed.\n  provenance: environment=developer-workstation-scratch-dir; principal=RebarBotNava; privilege_posture=narrower; instrument=simulation — the run reads a fresh clone of the tickets branch and publishes only to a file:// fake remote inside the scratch workdir.\n- [ ] `make verify` is run and any failure is fixed or shown environmental; the Gerrit change gets `Verified +1`; `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exit 0.\n\n## Portability\n\nThe harness uses core git and local file:// remotes only. It runs the same with or without a CI provider.\n"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1794874698791901553,"uuid":"6418cc6e-1152-44a4-bd3b-6bea87470099"}